# Control Circular Slider

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

The circular slider sets one value, or a low and high pair, by moving a handle round a dial. Home Assistant uses it for the target temperature of a thermostat, the target humidity of a humidifier or water heater, and the brightness of a light. People meet it in the Home Assistant dialogs and in the light card. It is not a card type, so you cannot write it in dashboard YAML.

This page covers:

- Control Circular Slider
  - `ha-control-circular-slider`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

- A 270 degree arc, open at the bottom, with a track behind it.
- A coloured arc from the start to the handle.
- A white round handle, the target.
- In some dialogs, a small marker for the current reading.
- A label and the value as text. Home Assistant places these beside or inside the dial, not as part of it.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-ha-control-circular-slider"><code>ha-control-circular-slider</code></h3>

- One value, or two for a low and high target (`dual`).
- Settings include a minimum (0 by default), a maximum (100), a step (1), `disabled` and `readonly`.
- A mode of `start`, `end` or `full` sets which side of the handle is coloured.
- The value is spoken and shown through a formatting function, so a unit can be added.

Keyboard: the arrow keys move by one step, Page Up and Page Down by a tenth of the range, and Home and End go to the ends. A tap on the arc also sets a value, so dragging is not the only way. The handle is exposed as a slider with a name, a minimum, a maximum and a value in words.

Variables in the Civica theme that it reads:

- `primary-color` for the coloured arc.
- `primary-text-color` for the current-reading marker.
- `clear-background-color` for the cleared part of the arc.

Things that stay as Home Assistant draws them:

- The track uses Home Assistant’s default disabled colour at low opacity. The Civica theme does not set `disabled-color`.
- The handle is white.
- The focus cue is the arc growing thicker. The Civica focus ring does not apply to it.

Changing the arc thickness, the track or the handle needs the card-mod integration or custom code, so test the exact selector in your own dashboard.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A dial is quick to set approximately on a touch screen.
- It is hard to set an exact value, and a pointer or finger that slips changes it.
- The arc alone does not say what the value is. Always show the number.
- You cannot use it outside the dialogs and card that Home Assistant builds it into.
- It does not take a typed value.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

- Show the target as text beside the dial, with its unit.
- Offer an exact alternative next to it: plus and minus buttons or a typed value. See [Control Number Box](control-number-box.html) and [Control Slider](sliders.html).
- Do not rely on colour to show how far the value is from normal.
- Give the dial a label that says what it sets: “Target temperature”.
- Do not run a device command on every small movement. The dialogs apply the value when the handle is released.

Success criteria that apply:

- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value and 2.1.1 Keyboard: the handle is a slider with a name and value, and the arrow keys work.
- 2.5.1 Pointer Gestures and 2.5.7 Dragging Movements: a tap on the arc and the keyboard are alternatives to dragging.
- 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast: the track and the arc need 3:1. Check the track in your own dashboard.
- 2.4.7 Focus Visible and 2.4.13 Focus Appearance (AAA): the thicker arc is the only cue. Check it in your own dashboard.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: show the value in text.

